  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Strong to severe storms ending this evening, \nquiet for Saturday, then another chance for severe weather on \nSunday.\n\nExpect a decreasing trend in storm strength this evening. A few\ndamaging wind gusts still possible in the Piedmont til 9pm.\n\nGoing to see drier weather overnight and some fog formation is\nlikely. Will have to monitor for potential dense fog.\n\nSaturday will be mostly quiet thanks to high pressure moving in\nfrom the north, pushing the cold front off to our south \nbriefly, though an isolated shower or storm will be possible in \nNorthwest North Carolina. The high begins to shift east on \nSunday, with another cold front approaching the area in the \nafternoon, again providing forcing for strong to severe storms, \nwith damaging winds as the main threat. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Above normal temperatures expected through \nSunday.\n\nWhile a more dry airmass pushes into the region, temperatures \non Saturday still look to be above normal, with highs in the \nlow 90s forecast for the Piedmont, and low to mid 80s for \nmountain locations. The drier airmass will make the air feel \nless muggy on Saturday; however, a return of higher dewpoints on\nSunday combined with increasing high temperatures back into the\nmid 90s for the Piedmont and mid to upper 80s for mountain \nlocations, will increase Heat Index values once again across the\nregion. \n\nWhile Heat Advisory criteria will likely not be met,\ntemperatures will be warm across the region, and sensitive\ngroups should avoid outdoor activity during the peak heating of\nthe day. Those that work outdoors should similarly take frequent\nbreaks indoors with AC, and drink plenty of fluids.",
